GHSA-9qww-pwc4-77qq (CVE-2026-89160): PCRE2 10.48 bounded the backward scans
over a UTF subject by the start of its checked part in pcre2_match() only.
pcre2_dfa_match() (its extended grapheme clusters \X, word boundaries and the
rewind of a lookbehind) and the JIT (\X, the count-back of Regional_Indicator
characters with BACKCHAR) still read before the start of the subject when it
was not checked as UTF there: a subject that begins with UTF-8 continuation
bytes (or 16-bit low surrogates) and a start offset into it, as a check of UTF
covers only the part a match can reach. A read before the buffer of the
caller (a crash when the page before it is not mapped): preg_match() of PHP
with /u, \X and an offset (the JIT), GRegex of GLib. Not fixed upstream (10.49
has the same code).

The patches of SUSE (Martin Schreiner, 10.09.2026, bsc#1277710, SLE 16 and
Leap 16.0 on 10.45), which apply to 10.49 as they are:
pcre2-dfa-match-check-subject.patch keeps the bound pcre2_dfa_match() checks
UTF from in the new dfa_match_block field check_subject, as match_block has it
since 10.34, and bounds the three by it (in non-UTF mode, and with
PCRE2_NO_UTF_CHECK, it is the start of the subject: nothing changes);
pcre2-jit-extuni-backchar-bound.patch makes the backward step of the JIT \X
stop at the start of the subject, as GETCHARBACK_INVALID() does (no change
for valid UTF). make check (8, 16 and 32 bits, JIT): passes.

